<!-- cell: title-page id="02047e99-2674-4894-bd7b-d5c2d6c4534f" title="The Same Set" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="5ec09543-b8ec-4924-8397-fe9eef70174d" -->

Every Thursday at four, Lia climbed the stairs of the Café Zsolnay to the back room where the old men played chess under a ceiling stained brown by a century of cigarettes, and every Thursday Benedek was already there, setting up the pieces.

He was eighty-one. He had taught mathematics for forty years and lost to almost no one in that room for longer than that. Lia was a philosophy student with no money and a stubborn Sicilian Defence, and he beat her every week with an air of quiet apology.

It was in her second winter that she noticed the pieces. The white king was boxwood, pale and smooth. The white queen was darker, almost amber. One black knight had a chipped ear, and one black rook was plainly plastic.

"Your set doesn't match," she said.

"It used to," said Benedek. "In 1961 it was perfect. Then a pawn went into a cup of coffee, and a bishop went home in a child's pocket, and the original king, I am sorry to say, went into the stove during a very cold February. I have replaced every piece. Not one of the first set is left."

"Then is it the same set?"

He smiled the way he smiled when she walked into a trap. "You tell me. You are the philosopher."

She thought about it while he took her bishop. "If it's the pieces, then no. But nobody here would say it's a different set. They'd say it's Benedek's set. So maybe a set is the games played on it, not the wood."

"Maybe," said Benedek. He was quiet for a while. Then, carefully, as if moving a piece he could not take back, he said: "The doctor says I am forgetting things. Names first. Then other things. He showed me pictures of my own brain. Pieces going, one by one."

Lia looked at the board, at the boxwood king and the plastic rook. Around them cups clinked and old men muttered over their clocks, as they had every afternoon since before either of them was born.

"I wonder," Benedek said, "if at the end there will be anyone left who is the same man. Or only someone sitting in his chair."

She did not have an answer, and he did not seem to expect one. They finished the game. He won, as always.

On the last Thursday of March he was not there. On the table was the chess set, in its old wooden box, and a card in his precise teacher's hand: For Lia. It is still the same set. You remember the games.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="c624b87f-1ab4-4277-a686-e9fe26728e3a" title="Benedek" words="43-65, 202-213, 394-403" -->

An eighty-one-year-old former mathematics teacher and chess player who is losing his memory, piece by piece, as his chess set once lost its pieces.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="86998379-7692-4c88-ac40-1b06eddd23ed" title="Lia" words="66-90, 221-229, 372-385" -->

A philosophy student who plays Benedek every Thursday, loses every week, and is left his chess set.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="004f4bc7-7736-492d-ab59-a607935a0cdd" title="The chess room of the Café Zsolnay" words="0-42, 324-346" -->

The back room of a Budapest café where old men have played chess for a century under a ceiling stained by smoke.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="68af13e0-14e2-4240-84d6-af374cde1442" title="Identity" words="196-201, 230-262, 347-371" -->

Whether something whose every part has been replaced is still the same thing: the chess set, and Benedek himself.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="90de95c6-4e30-4a3b-86ae-20bfd16c2d8b" title="Memory" words="286-297, 298-310, 426-435" -->

Benedek's illness: he is forgetting, names first, and the story never says the word for it.

<!-- cell: plot id="94b83d9d-907c-4433-8303-fd94a92f1f24" title="The same set" words="91-101, 131-136, 183-195, 196-201, 230-262, 286-310, 347-371, 394-435" -->

Lia notices the mismatched pieces; Benedek tells her every piece has been replaced and asks if it is the same set; she answers that it is the games; he reveals he is losing his memory; on the last Thursday he leaves her the set.
